    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "The four test scores are $11,15,18,24$.\n(a) Use a calculator to find the mean and population standard deviation. Give standard deviations correct to $2$ decimal places.\n(b) Instead, increase every original score by $3$. State the new mean and standard deviation.\n(c) Instead, decrease every original score by $5$. State the new mean and standard deviation.",
        "solution_text": "(a) Enter $11,15,18,24$ into statistics mode.\nRead $\\bar{x}=17$ and $\\sigma_x=4.743416\\ldots\\approx4.74$.\n(b) Each score and the mean increase by $3$.\nNew mean $=17+3=20$.\nThe spread is unchanged, so $\\sigma_x\\approx4.74$.\n(c) Each score and the mean decrease by $5$.\nNew mean $=17-5=12$.\nThe spread is unchanged, so $\\sigma_x\\approx4.74$.\nA shift changes the mean, not the standard deviation."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "The five scores $9,12,16,18,20$ have mean $15$ and population standard deviation $4$. Consider each change separately.\n(a) Add one new score of $15$. Explain what happens to the standard deviation.\n(b) Add one new score of $1$. Explain what happens to the standard deviation.\n(c) Replace all five original scores by $15$. State the new mean and explain why the standard deviation is zero.",
        "solution_text": "(a) The new score equals the mean, so its distance from the mean is $0$.\nThere is less spread overall, so the standard deviation decreases.\n(b) The new score is $15-1=14$ below the original mean.\nThis distance is much greater than the current standard deviation of $4$.\nAdding this score increases the spread, so the standard deviation increases.\n(c) Every score is now $15$, so the mean remains $15$.\nThere is no spread, so $\\sigma_x=0$."
      }
    ],
